<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Church of the Holy Sepulchre in the Old City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> stands on the site that Christian tradition holds to be the place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>'s crucifixion and burial. First built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> in the 4th century, it was destroyed on the orders of the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Hakim in 1009, partly rebuilt, and enlarged by the crusaders, who reconsecrated it in 1149. Several Christian churches share its custody today.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre.jpg" alt="Church of the Holy Sepulchre" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerd Eichmann · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_the_Holy_Sepulchre_by_Gerd_Eichmann_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 70 the city lay in ruins. In 130 the emperor Hadrian began building the Roman colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelia-capitolina">Aelia Capitolina</a>, and about 135 he ordered a cave containing a rock-cut tomb to be filled in as a flat foundation for a temple of Venus or Aphrodite. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> began to favour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, Bishop Macarius of Jerusalem asked permission to dig for the tomb. With the help of Eusebius, three crosses were found near a tomb, and one that was said to have cured the dying was taken to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a>.</p><p>The church, planned by the architect Zenobius, was built as two separate structures: a rotunda called the Anastasis, or Resurrection, over the tomb, and the great basilica known as the Martyrium, across a colonnaded courtyard to the east, with the traditional site of Calvary in one corner. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> places the tomb outside the city wall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> came under Islamic rule the early Muslim rulers protected its Christian sites. On 18 October 1009 the Fatimid caliph al-Hakim ordered its complete destruction, as part of a wider campaign against Christian and Jewish places of worship, and Christian Europe reacted with shock. His son allowed Emperor Constantine IX Monomachos to rebuild it, and the work was completed in 1048.</p><p>The knights of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> took the church on 15 July 1099, and it was modified into a structure that departs greatly from the original. It was lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> in 1187, though the treaty after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-crusade">Third Crusade</a> allowed Christian pilgrims to visit. Emperor Frederick II regained it by treaty in the 13th century, and the Khwarezmians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> captured it in 1244. An Ottoman decree of 1757 helped establish the Status Quo, confirmed by a firman of Sultan Abdülmecid I in 1852 and 1853.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The church is the seat of the Armenian, Greek Orthodox and Latin Catholic patriarchates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and it has been a major pilgrimage site since the fourth century. The main communities sharing it are the Roman Catholic, Greek Orthodox, Armenian Apostolic, Coptic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church">Ethiopian Orthodox</a>. Inside lie the last four stations of the Cross and Calvary, and the tomb is enclosed in the Aedicule, a 19th-century shrine. Disputes between the communities have at times led to violence: in 1902, 18 friars were hospitalized after a dispute over who could clean a step.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Church of the Holy Sepulchre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kings were usually crowned in the <b>Church of the Holy Sepulchre</b>, and accession required recognition by the High Court, the assembly of the king’s vassals, which also judged disputes among nobles and advised on war and law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>At a council in the <b>Church of the Holy Sepulchre</b>, after Raymond of Toulouse had refused the crown, Godfrey of Bouillon was elected to rule Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-22">22 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On the orders of the Fatimid caliph al-Hakim, the <b>Church of the Holy Sepulchre</b> in Jerusalem was torn down.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-18">18 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
